{"id":"cluster-administration-node-shutdown","corpus":"k8s","title":"Node Shutdowns","sourceUrl":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/cluster-administration/node-shutdown/","license":"Kubernetes Documentation © The Kubernetes Authors, licensed CC BY 4.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","blocks":[{"kind":"h1","text":"Node Shutdowns"},{"kind":"p","text":"In a Kubernetes cluster, a node can be shut down in a planned graceful way or unexpectedly because of reasons such as a power outage or something else external. A node shutdown could lead to workload failure if the node is not drained before the shutdown. A node shutdown can be either graceful or non-graceful."},{"kind":"p","text":"The unattended-upgrades package from Debian conflicts with node graceful shutdown in its normal configuration. If you use the default configuration of unattended-upgrades, which customizes the server shutdown grace period, then the kubelet fails to obtain the necessary lock to handle shutdown events properly."},{"kind":"p","text":"This happens if the shutdownGracePeriod value is greater than 30 seconds. To avoid this, you can suppress part of the unattended-upgrades configuration, by making /etc/systemd/logind.conf.d/unattended-upgrades-logind-maxdelay.conf be a symbolic link to /dev/null."},{"kind":"p","text":"For more details, refer to the logind.conf documentation."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Graceful node shutdown"},{"kind":"p","text":"The kubelet attempts to detect node system shutdown and terminates pods running on the node."},{"kind":"p","text":"Kubelet ensures that pods follow the normal pod termination process during the node shutdown. During node shutdown, the kubelet does not accept new Pods (even if those Pods are already bound to the node)."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Enabling graceful node shutdown"},{"kind":"p","text":"On Linux, the graceful node shutdown feature is controlled with the GracefulNodeShutdown feature gate which is enabled by default in 1.21."},{"kind":"p","text":"The graceful node shutdown feature depends on systemd since it takes advantage of systemd inhibitor locks to delay the node shutdown with a given duration."},{"kind":"p","text":"On Windows, the graceful node shutdown feature is controlled with the WindowsGracefulNodeShutdown feature gate which is introduced in 1.32 as an alpha feature. In Kubernetes 1.34 the feature is Beta and is enabled by default."},{"kind":"p","text":"The Windows graceful node shutdown feature depends on kubelet running as a Windows service, it will then have a registered service control handler to delay the preshutdown event with a given duration."},{"kind":"p","text":"Windows graceful node shutdown can not be cancelled."},{"kind":"p","text":"If kubelet is not running as a Windows service, it will not be able to set and monitor the Preshutdown event, the node will have to go through the Non-Graceful Node Shutdown procedure mentioned above."},{"kind":"p","text":"In the case where the Windows graceful node shutdown feature is enabled, but the kubelet is not running as a Windows service, the kubelet will continue running instead of failing. However, it will log an error indicating that it needs to be run as a Windows service."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Configuring graceful node shutdown"},{"kind":"p","text":"Note that by default, both configuration options described below, shutdownGracePeriod and shutdownGracePeriodCriticalPods, are set to zero, thus not activating the graceful node shutdown functionality. To activate the feature, both options should be configured appropriately and set to non-zero values."},{"kind":"p","text":"Once the kubelet is notified of a node shutdown, it sets a NotReady condition on the Node, with the reason set to \"node is shutting down\". The kube-scheduler honors this condition and does not schedule any Pods onto the affected node; other third-party schedulers are expected to follow the same logic. This means that new Pods won't be scheduled onto that node and therefore none will start."},{"kind":"p","text":"The kubelet also rejects Pods during the PodAdmission phase if an ongoing node shutdown has been detected, so that even Pods with a toleration for node.kubernetes.io/not-ready:NoSchedule do not start there."},{"kind":"p","text":"When kubelet is setting that condition on its Node via the API, the kubelet also begins terminating any Pods that are running locally."},{"kind":"p","text":"During a graceful shutdown, kubelet terminates pods in two phases:"},{"kind":"li","text":"Terminate regular pods running on the node."},{"kind":"li","text":"Terminate critical pods running on the node."},{"kind":"p","text":"The graceful node shutdown feature is configured with two KubeletConfiguration options:"},{"kind":"li","text":"shutdownGracePeriod: Specifies the total duration that the node should delay the shutdown by. This is the total grace period for pod termination for both regular and critical pods."},{"kind":"li","text":"shutdownGracePeriodCriticalPods: Specifies the duration used to terminate critical pods during a node shutdown. This value should be less than shutdownGracePeriod."},{"kind":"p","text":"There are cases when Node termination was cancelled by the system (or perhaps manually by an administrator). In either of those situations the Node will return to the Ready state. However, Pods which already started the process of termination will not be restored by kubelet and will need to be re-scheduled."}],"lemmas":{"nodes":["node","nod","nodes"],"shutdowns":["shutdown","shutdowns"],"kubernetes":["kubernetes","kubernete"],"clusters":["cluster","clusters"],"clustering":["clustering","cluster"],"clustered":["clustered","cluster"],"shuts":["shuts","shut"],"downs":["down","downs"],"downing":["downing","down"],"downed":["downed","down"],"planned":["planned","plan"],"planneds":["planneds","planned"],"plans":["plan","plans"],"planning":["planning","plan"],"planed":["planed","plan"],"gracefuls":["gracefuls","graceful"],"unexp